            "text": "{\n  \"verdict\": \"ACCEPTED\",\n  \"findings\": [],\n  \"missing_assumptions\": [],\n  \"counterexamples\": [],\n  \"limitations\": [\n    \"The derivation does not supply the native pair-weight direction mu or the residual delta.\",\n    \"The physical identification of the historical support current with J or 2J remains unproved.\",\n    \"The result does not imply source-event-receiver access, native bit readout, or a physical interaction/law.\",\n    \"The derivation applies to a fixed ledger and does not represent moving carriers, births/deaths, or changes of product.\"\n  ],\n  \"reconstruction\": [\n    \"The exact coefficient variation of the quadratic functional I_w(K) is t F_mu(K), where F_mu(K) = sum_a mu_a h(K_a, K_a).\",\n    \"Polarizing F_mu yields the unique symmetric bilinear form B_mu(K, L) = sum_a mu_a h(K_a, L_a).\",\n    \"Evaluating one slot at the actual residual delta defines the linear functional J_{mu, delta}(K) = sum_a mu_a h(delta_a, K_a).\",\n    \"In operator notation, J = E^* D_mu G E delta, which respects all evaluation relations and avoids extending to undeclared pairs.\",\n    \"The baseline bilinear form B_w induces a Riesz map R_w. The null space N_w of the baseline quadratic seminorm is exactly ker R_w.\",\n    \"The covector J descends to the quotient V/N_w if and only if J(N_w) = 0, which is equivalent to J in im R_w.\",\n    \"A sufficient condition for descent is supp(mu) subset {a : w_a \u003e 0}, as demonstrated by the positive-semidefinite property of h.\"\n  ],\n  \"constructive_analysis\": [\n    \"The derivation correctly distinguishes between the polar lift J and the residual derivative 2J, preventing silent identification.\",\n    \"The counterexample in Section 4 rigorously proves that the scalar action identity F_mu(delta) is insufficient to uniquely recover the covector J.\",\n    \"The counterexample in Section 5 correctly bounds the quotient claim, showing that automatic descent fails for on